      "created_at": "2025-11-10T10:23:32Z",
      "updated_at": "2025-11-10T10:24:37Z",
      "body": "I think the issue comes from how the error handler return value is accounted for in the unicode-escape / raw-unicode-escape decoder path.\n\nWhen the custom error handler returns a replacement string and also rewinds the input (so that more input remains to be processed), the decoder needs to ensure there is enough room for:\n\n1. The entire replacement string, and\n2. The newly increased \"remaining input\" that will be decoded next.\n\nCurrently, the helper only adds (replacement_length - 1) to the required buffer size. That works only if the caller already pre-reserved space for exactly one output character, which isn’t true here. So in cases where the replacement length is exactly 1 and the input is rewound, the buffer ends up short by 1 character → leading to the assertion failure.\n\nThe correct behavior is to always account for the full replacement length when updating writer.min_length, along with any delta in remaining input. That ensures the writer capacity stays consistent with the decoder’s assumptions across all callers.",
      "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
