      "body": "@serhiy-storchaka I think the core idea here is to avoid growing the buffer every time and instead only do so when it is actually needed. The goal is to keep the assertion safe when the error handler rewinds the position, without introducing unnecessary overallocation in the normal path.\n\nThe way I am thinking about it is:\n\n```\nrequired = writer->pos + replen + new_remaining;\nif (required > writer->min_length) {\n    writer->min_length = required;\n}\n```\n\nSo the buffer only grows when this lower bound is larger than the current minimum. In cases where replen == 1 and there is no rewind, it should not trigger any extra allocation. And when the error handler rewinds input, this should still ensure the writer has enough room so the assertion remains valid.\n\nOn the infinite loop concern with rewinding, I agree that is a broader behavioral question. Here I am only focusing on making sure the writer capacity stays consistent so the crash does not occur. I may be missing something.",
