      "body": "@mohsinm-dev , your changes still give an infinite loop:\n\n```\nb'\\\\x0n\\\\z' 3\n(b'\\xe2\\x98\\x83', 0)\nb'\\\\x0n\\\\z' 3\n(b'\\xe2\\x98\\x83', 0)\nb'\\\\x0n\\\\z' 3\n(b'\\xe2\\x98\\x83', 0)\nb'\\\\x0n\\\\z' 3\n(b'\\xe2\\x98\\x83', 0)\nb'\\\\x0n\\\\z' 3\n(b'\\xe2\\x98\\x83', 0)\nb'\\\\x0n\\\\z' 3\n(b'\\xe2\\x98\\x83', 0)\nb'\\\\x0n\\\\z' 3\n(b'\\xe2\\x98\\x83', 0)\nb'\\\\x0n\\\\z' 3\n(b'\\xe2\\x98\\x83', 0)\nb'\\\\x0n\\\\z' 3\n^C(b'\\xe2\\x98\\x83', 0)\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"/home/abhishek/Python/filescpy/test_141336\", line 18, in <module>\n    result, length = decode(input_obj, 'test.mutating2')\n                     ~~~~~~^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n  File \"/home/abhishek/Python/filescpy/test_141336\", line 11, in mutating\n    print(r)\n    ~~~~~^^^\nKeyboardInterrupt\n```\n\nSince the problem is that the code rewinds back to the start of the string, which is mostly due to `newpos=0` in the `unicode_decode_call_errorhandler_writer()`, can't we simply add a guard to prevent non-progressing situations? Like:\n\n```\n\nPy_ssize_t prev_resume_offset = -1;\n\n{\n    Py_ssize_t cur_resume_offset = s - starts;\n    if (prev_resume_offset >= 0 && cur_resume_offset <= prev_resume_offset) {\n        PyErr_SetString(PyExc_UnicodeError,\n            \"decoding error handler returned non-progressing position\");\n        goto onError;\n    }\n    prev_resume_offset = cur_resume_offset;\n}\n```",
